const t=6,e="The Effect of Iron Deficiency and Anemia on Women's Health",i="Priyanka Maurya, Pavan Kumar, Asha Rani",n=[80,88],a=["1 Department of Zoology,","Tilak Dhari P.G. College, Jaunpur, Affiliated to V. B. S. Purvanchal","University, Jaunpur, Uttar Pradesh, India 222002","2 Faculty of Engineering and Technology, Datta Meghe Institute of Higher Education and","Research (DMIHER), DU, Wardha-442001, India","*Corresponding author-Dr. Asha Rani","*E-mail - anshuaucky@gmail.com"],o=[{t:"h",x:"ABSTRACT :"},{t:"p",x:"Iron deficiency and anemia are major global health problems, affecting approximately 1 billion people. Iron deficiency is the most common cause, responsible for 50% of all anemia cases. It mostly affects children, women and the elderly. Additionally, it often occurs alongside chronic conditions such as kidney disease, heart failure and inflammatory bowel disease. Iron plays an important role in oxygen transport, cellular metabolism, and immune function. Anemia occurs when the body lacks sufficient iron or cannot absorb it properly. This deficiency leads to reduced haemoglobin (Hb) synthesis, resulting in reduced oxygen carrying capacity and tissue hypoxia. Symptoms include extreme tiredness, weakness, pale skin, impaired immunity, and less ability to do physical work. Many women suffer from these conditions due to menstrual blood loss, heavy uterine bleeding, and pregnancy. In women, chronic iron deficiency is associated with adverse reproductive outcomes, including infertility, premature birth, low birth weight, and high maternal morbidity. During pregnancy iron deficiency (ID) can harm both the mother and the baby. Infants born to iron deficient mothers may face challenges with brain development and learning ability. Early diagnosis and awareness are important. Doctors typically diagnose the condition by checking hemoglobin (Hb) and serum ferritin levels in the blood. Hepcidin plays a key role in iron homeostasis and could be a future diagnostic and therapeutic target. Treatment options include iron supplements, which can be given by orally or through injection intravenously. Keywords: Cellular metabolism, Haemoglobin (Hb), Hepcidin, Homeostasis, Hypoxia, Infertility, Intravenous."},{t:"h",x:"1. Introduction:"},{t:"p",x:"Iron deficiency and anemia are global health problems widely observed in daily life. Iron deficiency (ID) is the most common nutritional deficiency globally, affecting approximately 25% of the population, roughly 2 billion people (Pasricha, et al., 2021). It is estimated to affect up to 40% of people in developing countries and 10% in developed countries, making it the leading cause of anemia worldwide."},{t:"p",x:"Anemia is characterized by a decreased number of red blood cells or low concentration of hemoglobin (Hb). Because hemoglobin transports oxygen throughout the body, a reduction can lead to weakness, fatigue,"},{t:"h",x:"CHAPTER 6"},{t:"h",x:"The Effect of Iron Deficiency and Anemia on Women's Health"},{t:"p",x:"reduced immunity, and low productivity. According to the World Health Organization (WHO), anemia affects about 30% of non-pregnant women, 37% of pregnant women, and 40% of children under five years old (WHO/UNICEF, 2025)."},{t:"p",x:"While other conditions like malaria, thalassemia, and sickle cell anemia also contribute to the global burden, iron deficiency (ID) remains a primary cause. Iron is essential for synthesis of hemoglobin. When iron deficiency becomes severe, it leads to iron deficiency anemia (IDA), which is the most common form of anemia. Currently, more than 1.2 billion people suffer from iron deficiency anemia (IDA), a condition prevalent in both developing and developed countries due to unhealthy dietary patterns (Kassebaum, et al., 2014; Gardner, et al., 2023)."},{t:"h",x:"2. Etiology of Iron Deficiency Anemia:"},{t:"p",x:"Iron-deficiency anemia (IDA) occurs when the body lacks sufficient iron to produce healthy red blood cells. It can occur due to low iron intake, impaired iron absorption, or chronic blood loss. The prevalence of these underlying causes often varies based on age, gender, economic condition and social factors. Chronic blood loss: In older adults, the most common cause of iron-deficiency anemia (IDA) is chronic blood loss. Even a small amount of consistent bleeding can reduce iron storage over time. Main causes of blood loss include stomach or intestinal cancer, peptic ulcer disease, inflammatory bowel disease, celiac disease, and several problems in the digestive tract. Blood loss may also occur due to heavy menstrual bleeding in women, or other non-digestive sources."},{t:"p",x:"Increased physiological demand: Iron-deficiency anemia can happen when the body requires more iron than normal conditions, such as during pregnancy, infancy, and adolescence. If the body does not obtain sufficient iron from food or can not absorb it properly, it can cause anemia. Some health problems such as celiac disease, stomach inflammation, and weight loss surgery can significantly decrease iron absorption (Auerbach M, et al., 2025). During pregnancy, the mother's body requires more nourishment and blood to help in the developing fetus. While blood plasma volume increases by 40-50% , and red blood blood cells (RBC) also increase by only 20-30%. Because of all these changes blood becomes diluted, resulting in the physiologic anemia of pregnancy (Sifakis S, et al., 2000)."},{t:"p",x:"Chronic inflammatory conditions, including long-term kidney disease, heart failure, and inflammatory bowel disease, can decrease the body's capacity to absorb or utilize iron. This occurs because of a hormone called hepcidin, which blocks the release of stored iron and decreases its availability for red blood cell synthesis. This leads to what is often termed the anemia of chronic disease or IDA (Auerbach M, et al., 2025). Additionally, IDA is common in infants and young children who are not breastfed and instead consume excessive cow's milk."},{t:"p",x:"In developing countries, iron-deficiency anemia is frequently caused by parasitic infections (like hookworm), malaria, and inherited blood disorders. These issues are often compounded by malnutrition and chronic disease (Brittenham GM, et al., 2023). Additionally, a bacteria that is Helicobacter pylori infection and autoimmune gastritis can impair iron absorption, often making the primary cause difficult to diagnose. In rare cases, individuals are born with genetic disorders affecting iron metabolism, such as iron-refractory iron-deficiency anemia (IRIDA). In these instances, standard iron treatments are often ineffective (Khan L. 2018; Brissot P, et al., 2018; Zohora F, et al., 2018; Gattermann N, et al., 2021)."},{t:"h",x:"3. Epidemiology:"},{t:"p",x:"According to the Global Burden of Disease study 2021, disability-adjusted life years (DALYs) for iron deficiency remained extremely high from 1990 to 2021. Consequently, iron deficiency has become a major global health challenge, particularly in low-income regions (Huang S, et al., 2025). According to the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES), approximately 14% of adults in the United States suffer from iron-deficiency anemia (Auerbach M, et al., 2025). In India, the National Family Health Survey-6 (2023- 24) reports that approximately 52% of women (ages 15-49) and 58% of children under five years suffer from anemia. These high rates are primarily attributed to poor nutrition, inadequate sanitation, and limited access to healthcare."},{t:"h",x:"4. Pathophysiology of Iron-Deficiency Anemia:"},{t:"p",x:"Iron-Deficiency anemia (IDA) occurs when hemoglobin (Hb) production is reduced because the body lacks a sufficient iron supply for red blood cell formation. The process begins with the depletion of iron stores (ferritin), which are primarily located in the liver and the reticuloendothelial system, which acts as the body's main reservoir. As these stores are exhausted, the level of circulating iron in the bloodstream (transferrin saturation) also declines. Consequently, the bone marrow cannot produce sufficient amounts of hemoglobin to form healthy red blood cells (Li X, et al., 2025; Li N, et al., 2020)."},{t:"p",x:"Iron is an essential component for the body, as it is vital for the formation of hemoglobin, which transports oxygen in the blood. The body obtains iron primarily through dietary intake, intestinal absorption, and the recycling of iron from senescent red blood cells (Shah Y, et al., 2021). Dietary iron exists in two forms: heme and non-heme iron. Heme iron is obtained from animal food sources like meat, poultry and fish and is easily absorbed by the body. In contrast, non-heme iron is found mainly in plant based foods such as vegetables, grains, and pulses, and is less readily absorbed. Certain compounds in plants including phytates, oxalates, polyphenols, and tannins, can inhibit iron absorption. Additionally, medication such as proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) used for acidity also reduced iron absorption (Hallberg L, 1987; Disler PB, 1975). Conversely, substances such as ascorbic acid (vitamin C), citrate, and gastric acid enhance absorption (Gulec S, et al., 2014). Although daily dietary iron consumption typically ranges from 5-15 mg, the body's actual net absorption is quite low, totaling only about 1-2 mg. This absorption occurs primarily in the small intestine, predominantly within the duodenum and proximal jejunum (Monsen ER, et al., 1978)."},{t:"h",x:"5. Clinical Manifestation and Diagnosis:"},{t:"p",x:"According to the World Health Organization (WHO), anemia is defined as a condition in which the hemoglobin (Hb) level is lower than that of a healthy individual. Specifically, if Hb levels are below 130/gL in men and 120/gL in women, they are considered anaemic (World Health Organization, 2011). Anemia cannot be diagnosed by observing symptoms alone; blood tests are required for confirmation. While fatigue is the most prevalent symptom, it is non-specific; in one study, only one out of 52 patients presenting with fatigue actually had anemia, while the others suffered from stress, lifestyle, or related problems (Elnicki DM, et al., 1992). Advanced clinical signs of iron-deficiency anemia such as koilonychia (spoon shaped nails), glossitis, and dysphagia are rarely seen in developed countries because iron deficiency is usually identified and treated before it becomes severe (Cook JD, 2005)."},{t:"p",x:"The diagnosis of iron-deficiency anemia involves several laboratory tests. While each test provides accurate information, no single result is sufficient on its own; results must be evaluated alongside the patient's symptoms, medical history, and overall health. Routine tests used in the diagnosis of iron-deficiency anemia (IDA) including serum ferritin, Total iron binding capacity (TIBC), transferrin saturation. Additionally, advanced diagnostic markers such as soluble transferrin receptor (sTfR), hepcidin levels, and reticulocyte hemoglobin concentration may be utilized for a more comprehensive assessment (Hillman S, et al.)."},{t:"h",x:"6. Complications and Comorbidities of IDA"},{t:"p",x:"If iron-deficiency anemia (IDA) remains untreated for an extended period, it can lead to multi-organ dysfunction, affecting the hematologic, neurologic, cardiovascular, epithelial, reproductive, and immunological systems. Neurological complications include restless legs syndrome, depression, and chronic headache. In rare cases, long-term IDA can lead to Plummer-Vinson syndrome, characterized by dysphagia (difficulty swallowing). Cardiovascular complications include tachycardia, functional systolic murmurs, and the exacerbation of heart failure; in extreme cases, this can lead to cardiovascular collapse. Furthermore, the immune system is often compromised, increasing the patient's susceptibility to infections. In women, IDA may contribute to infertility and difficulties in conceiving pregnancy. In children, it can significantly impair neurodevelopment, leading to poor attention spans, diminished academic performance, and delayed growth (Auerbach M, et al., 2025; Goyal A, et al., 2020; Musallam KM, et al., 2018)."},{t:"p",x:"Comorbidities: Iron-deficiency anemia (IDA) rarely occurs in isolation. It is frequently a secondary condition complicated by other medical issues. Mostly comorbidities such as chronic kidney disease (CKD), that present in approximately 40% of patients (Shiba, et al., 2011), diabetes, seen in nearly 30-40% of patients (Komajda, M. 2018), and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, which affects 20.5% of patients, it is long term lung disease (Rutten, F.H., et al., 2005). In all these conditions the risk of mortality increases. Inflammatory Bowel Disease: Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) is defined as a group of chronic conditions affecting the digestive system, including Crohn's disease (CD) and ulcerative colitis (UC). It can cause symptoms to get worse and then improve for some time. More than 25% of patients suffer from extraintestinal complications (Calvo P, et al., 2013). One of the most common problems is anemia in case of IBD (Hindryckx P, et al., 2014). Approximately one in three individuals with IBD has a hemoglobin level below 12 g/dL (Oldenburg B, et al., 2001). IBD is a long term intestinal disease, and anemia is a frequent clinical observation in these patients (Crawford J, 2002)."},{t:"p",x:"Heart Failure: Iron deficiency is mostly prevalent among patients with heart failure (HF). It can occur in anyone male or female, with or without anemia, and left ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF) (Yeo TJ, et al., 2014; Martens P, et al., 2017). Approximately half of all heart failure patients suffer from functional iron deficiency. In these cases the body contains iron stores but cannot utilize them properly, even if hemoglobin (Hb) levels remain within the normal range (Cappellini MD, et al., 2017; Klip IT, et al., 2013). Chronic Kidney Disease: Anemia is highly prevalent among individuals with chronic kidney disease (CKD). It happens due to irregular serum creatinine levels in the bloodstream (Hsu CY, et al., 2002). It usually occurs when the kidney is not working properly and estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) falls 60 mL/min or less. The condition is often driven by a deficiency in erythropoietin production rather than just irregular creatinine levels. CKD becomes significantly more severe when followed by untreated anemia. Furthermore, patients with diabetes frequently experience more severe anemia than non-diabetic patients at the same stage of kidney disease. (El-Achkar TM, et al., 2005)."},{t:"p",x:"Impact on Immune System: Iron plays a vital role in biological life. It facilitates essential cellular activities such as DNA synthesis, ATP production, and mitochondrial respiration (Aisen P, et al., 2001). When harmful pathogens enter the body, then the body tries to protect itself by reducing the levels of free iron. This occurs due to several bacteria and other pathogens required to grow and survive (Johnson EE, et al., 2012). Experimental and clinical data suggest when the body is iron deficient, the risk of infection increases. Because iron is very crucial for the immune system, which protects the body from pathogens and disease. If iron levels are insufficient in the body, immune cells can not work properly. This makes it difficult for the body to fight against pathogens (Hershko C, 1996). As well as reduced ability to kill pathogens, weakened barrier protection, and poor oxygen transport."},{t:"h",x:"7. Prevention of IDA :"},{t:"p",x:"Historically, anemia prevention for women mostly focused on preventing iron deficiency and iron deficiency anemia (IDA) during pregnancy. This focus exists because anemia remains a leading cause of maternal mortality and other complications such as low birth weight, preterm birth, and other neonatal issues, mainly in developing countries (Zhao J., et al., 2022). Additionally, these issues do not only affect pregnant women, they also impact non-pregnant women. Women with iron deficiency often experience increased fatigue, reduced work capacity, and impaired intellectual and cognitive function (Coad J., et al., 2014). Prioritize Iron-Rich Foods: There are two types of dietary iron that the body can absorb: heme and non-heme. Heme Iron, this is obtained from animal food sources such as meat, poultry, fish, and seafood. It is derived from the hemoglobin and myoglobin proteins, which are present in animal tissue. About 15-35% of this form is easily absorbed by the body."},{t:"p",x:"Non-heme, this is derived from plant food sources and iron-fortified foods such as spinach and beans. This type is not as easily absorbed as heme iron."},{t:"p",x:"Avoid Iron Inhibitors: Certain dietary habits can block iron absorption. Try to avoid consuming these things, which can reduce iron absorption. Inhibitors of iron absorption include - Phytates, a compound which is found in plant-based foods like grains and seeds. It reduced iron absorption. Polyphenols are found in tea, coffee, wine, fruit, vegetables, cereals, and legumes. These antioxidants make it harder for the body to absorb iron. Calcium found in milk and dairy products. It also reduces absorption of both heme and non-heme iron. Certain animal proteins such as casein, whey, and egg whites, as well as plant proteins like soy protein may also interfere with iron uptake. Oxalic acid is found in spinach, beans, nuts, and chard, oxalic acid binds with iron and inhibits its absorption."},{t:"p",x:"Enhancer of Iron Absorption: Certain substances help the body absorb iron more effectively. Vitamin C is one of them, which helps in improving the absorption of non-heme iron. It can even reduce the harmful effects of substances that inhibit iron absorption. Vitamin C is easily obtained from plant food sources such as lemons, oranges, and other citrus fruits."},{t:"p",x:"Intermittent supplementation has been shown to be significantly more effective than no supplementation at all. In many cases, it is nearly as effective as daily supplementation for increasing iron levels in the body (Allen LH, 2002; Mozaffari-Khosravi H, et al., 2010)."},{t:"h",x:"8. Treatment:"},{t:"p",x:"The treatment of iron-deficiency anemia (IDA) mainly targeted two goals: replenishing iron stores to normalize hemoglobin levels and identifying the underlying cause of the deficiency. Oral Iron Supplementation: Oral iron is the first line treatment of iron-deficiency anemia (IDA). These supplements are easy to administer and highly effective. Several iron formulations are available without a prescription (Gasche C, et al., 2015; Rayani S, 2016). Among them, ferrous sulfate is the most common first choice because it is effective and well absorbed by the body (Santiago P, 2012). Therapeutic doses are significantly higher than those found in daily multivitamins. While iron is best absorbed on an empty stomach, taking it with a small amount of food is better than not taking it at all, if the supplement causes nausea or cramps."},{t:"p",x:"Intravenous Iron: If oral supplements fail or are ineffective, then intravenous (IV) infusion may be necessary. IV iron delivers iron directly into the bloodstream through a vein. This method is utilized when oral tablets are insufficient such as for individuals with malabsorption issues, inflammatory bowel disease, chronic blood loss, or end stage renal disease, or for those who cannot tolerate the side effects of oral iron. IV iron is not prescribed for everyone, as it can be more expensive and may not be easily available everywhere (Munoz M, et al., 2018)."},{t:"h",x:"9. Public Health Strategies:"},{t:"p",x:"According to the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI), public health policies for iron deficiency anemia (IDA) involve many different sectors working together. Their main goal is to decrease the worldwide impact of anemia, which affects more than 2 billion people. According to the World Health Organization (WHO) has set a target for 2025 to decrease anemia in women of reproductive age by 50%. In India, the government has launched programs such as Anemia Mukt Bharat (AMB), which was introduced in 2018 by the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare. This program transitioned from the older, National Iron Plus Initiative to the more aggressive AMB strategy."},{t:"h",x:"10. Conclusion:"},{t:"p",x:"For most individuals with simple iron deficiency (ID) and no other health issues, oral iron is the best option because it is easily available, inexpensive, effective, safe, and convenient. However, intravenous (IV) iron is better for patients who cannot tolerate the side effects of oral iron or have conditions where oral iron is likely to be ineffective or harmful, the intravenous (IV) route is preferred. Except for patients with ID associated with chemotherapy or dialysis, a single IV infusion of a replacement dose offers significant convenience and cost benefit. Research strongly shows that IV iron is a safe and important tool that should be used more often in modern medicine. Finally, because neonates born to iron deficient mothers face serious health risks, providing IV iron during pregnancy is an important medical need that should not be overlooked."},{t:"h",x:"References:"},{t:"p",x:"Aisen P, Enns C, Wessling-Resnick M. Chemistry and Biology of Eukaryotic Iron Metabolism. Int J Biochem Cell Biol (2001) 33(10):940–59. doi: 10.1016/S1357-2725(01)00063-2."},{t:"p",x:"Allen LH. Iron supplements: scientific issues concerning efficacy and implications for research and programs. Journal of Nutrition. 2002;132:813S–819S."},{t:"p",x:"Auerbach M, DeLoughery TG, Tirnauer JS. Iron Deficiency in Adults: A Review. JAMA. 2025 May 27;333(20):1813-1823."},{t:"p",x:"Brissot P, Bernard DG, Brissot E, Loréal O, Troadec MB. Rare anemias due to genetic iron metabolism defects. Mutat Res Rev Mutat Res. 2018 Jul-Sep;777:52-63."},{t:"p",x:"Brittenham GM, Moir-Meyer G, Abuga KM, Datta-Mitra A, Cerami C, Green R, Pasricha SR, Atkinson SH. Biology of Anemia: A Public Health Perspective. J Nutr. 2023 Dec;153 Suppl 1(Suppl 1):S7-S28."},{t:"p",x:"Calvo P, Pablo L. Managing IBD outside the gut: ocular manifestations. Dig Dis. 2013;31:229–232. doi: 10.1159/000353375."},{t:"p",x:"Cappellini MD, Comin-Colet J, de Francisco A, Dignass A, Doehner W, Lam CS, Macdougall IC, Rogler G, Camaschella C, Kadir R, Kassebaum NJ, Spahn DR, Taher AT, Musallam KM; IRON CORE Group. Iron deficiency across chronic inflammatory conditions: international expert opinion on definition, diagnosis, and management. Am J Hematol. 2017;92:1068–1078. doi: 10.1002/ajh.24820."},{t:"p",x:"Coad J., Pedley K. Iron deficiency and iron deficiency anemia in women. Scand. J. Clin. Lab. Investig. Suppl. 2014;244:82–89. doi: 10.3109/00365513.2014.936694"},{t:"p",x:"Cook JD. Diagnosis and management of iron-deficiency anaemia. Best Pract Res Clin Haematol. 2005;18:319- 32."},{t:"p",x:"Crawford J. Anemia and lung cancer. Lung Cancer. 2002;38 Suppl 3:S75–S78. doi: 10.1016/s0169- 5002(02)00275-1."},{t:"p",x:"Disler PB, Lynch SR, Charlton RW, et al. The effect of tea on iron absorption. Gut 1975; 16:193-200. doi:10.1136/gut.16.3.193."},{t:"p",x:"El-Achkar TM, Ohmit SE, McCullough PA, et al. Higher prevalence of anemia with diabetes mellitus in moderate kidney insufficiency: The Kidney Early Evaluation Program. Kidney Int. 2005; 67: 1483–1488. Elnicki DM, Shockcor WT, Brick JE, Beynon D. Evaluating the complaint of fatigue in primary care: diagnoses and outcomes. Am J Med. 1992;93:303-6."},{t:"p",x:"Gardner, W.M.; Razo, C.; McHugh, T.A.; Hagins, H.; Vilchis-Tella, V.M.; Hennessy, C.; Taylor, H.J.; Perumal, N.; Fuller, K.; Cercy, K.M.; et al. Prevalence, Years Lived with Disability, and Trends in Anaemia Burden by Severity and Cause, 1990–2021: Findings from the Global Burden of Disease Study 2021. Lancet Haematol. 2023, 10, e713–e734."},{t:"p",x:"Gasche C, Ahmad T, Tulassay Z, et al. Ferric maltol is effective in correcting iron deficiency anemia in patients with inflammatory bowel disease: results from a phase-3 clinical trial program. Inflamm Bowel Dis. 2015; 21(3): 579-588."},{t:"p",x:"Gattermann N, Muckenthaler MU, Kulozik AE, Metzgeroth G, Hastka J. The Evaluation of Iron Deficiency and Iron Overload. Dtsch Arztebl Int. 2021 Dec 10;118(49):847-856."},{t:"p",x:"Goyal A, Zheng Y, Albenberg LG, Stoner NL, Hart L, Alkhouri R, Hampson K, Ali S, Cho-Dorado M, Goyal RK, Grossman A. Anemia in Children With Inflammatory Bowel Disease: A Position Paper by the IBD Committee of the North American Society of Pediatric Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition. J Pediatr Gastroenterol Nutr. 2020 Oct;71(4):563-582."},{t:"p",x:"Gulec S, Anderson GJ, Collins JF, et al. Mechanistic and regulatory aspects of intestinal iron absorption. Am J Physiol Gastrointest Liver Physiol 2014; 307:G397–409. doi:10.1152/ajpgi.00348.2013. Hallberg L, Rossander L, Skånberg AB, et al. Phytates and the inhibitory effect of bran on iron absorption in man. Am J Clin Nutr 1987;45:988-96. doi:10.1093/ajcn/45.5.988."},{t:"p",x:"Hershko C. Iron and infection, Iron Nutr. Health Dis, 22 (1996), pp. 231-238."},{t:"p",x:"Hillman S, Finch C. Red Cell Manual. Transfusion, 5th ed. John Wiley and Sons. pp 1537–2995. Hindryckx P, Amininejad L, Van De Vijver E, Bossuyt P. Belgian recommendations for the management of anemia in patients with inflammatory bowel disease. Acta Gastroenterol Belg. 2014;77:333–344."},{t:"p",x:"Hsu CY, McCulloch CE, Curhan GC. Epidemiology of anemia associated with chronic renal insufficiency among adults in the United States: Results from the Third National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey. J Am Soc Nephrol. 2002; 13: 504–510."},{t:"p",x:"Huang S, Li H, Zhang L, Chen H, Gao C. Global Burden of Disease Associated With Iron Deficiency: GBD 2021. Int J Vitam Nutr Res. 2025 May 14;95(3):31351."},{t:"p",x:"Johnson EE, Wessling-Resnick M. Iron Metabolism and the Innate Immune Response to Infection. Microbes Infect (2012) 14(3):207–16. doi: 10.1016/j.micinf.2011.10.001."},{t:"p",x:"Kassebaum, N.J.; Jasrasaria, R.; Naghavi, M.; Wulf, S.K.; Johns, N.; Lozano, R.; Regan, M.; Weatherall, D.; Chou, D.P.; Eisele, T.P.; et al. A Systematic Analysis of Global Anemia Burden from 1990 to 2010. Blood 2014, 123, 615-624."},{t:"p",x:"Khan L. Anemia in Childhood. Pediatr Ann. 2018 Feb 01;47(2):e42-e47."},{t:"p",x:"Klip IT, Comin-Colet J, Voors AA, Ponikowski P, Enjuanes C, Banasiak W, Lok DJ, Rosentryt P, Torrens A, Polonski L, van Veldhuisen DJ, van der Meer P, Jankowska EA. Iron deficiency in chronic heart failure: an international pooled analysis. Am Heart J. 2013;165:575–582.e3. doi: 10.1016/j.ahj.2013.01.017. Komajda, M. Diabète et Insuffisance Cardiaque: Données Épidémiologiques et Implications Thérapeutiques. Bull Acad. Natl. Med. 2018, 202, 909–916."},{t:"p",x:"Li N, Zhao G, Wu W, Zhang M, Liu W, Chen Q, Wang X. The Efficacy and Safety of Vitamin C for Iron Supplementation in Adult Patients With Iron Deficiency Anemia: A Randomized Clinical Trial. JAMA Netw Open. 2020 Nov 02;3(11):e2023644."},{t:"p",x:"Li X, Finberg KE. Iron Deficiency Anemia. Adv Exp Med Biol. 2025;1480:163-178."},{t:"p",x:"Martens P, Nijst P, Verbrugge FH, Smeets K, Dupont M, Mullens W. Impact of iron deficiency on exercise capacity and outcome in heart failure with reduced, mid-range and preserved ejection fraction. Acta Cardiol. 2017;73:1–9. doi: 10.1080/00015385.2017.1351239."},{t:"p",x:"Monsen ER, Hallberg L, Layrisse M, et al. Estimation of available dietary iron. Am J Clin Nutr 1978; 31:134– 41. doi:10.1093/ajcn/31.1.134."},{t:"p",x:"Mozaffari-Khosravi H, et al. Once weekly low-dose iron supplementation effectively improved iron status in adolescent girls. Biological Trace Element Research. 2010;135:22–30."},{t:"p",x:"Munoz M, Gomez-Ramirez S, Bhandari S. The safety of available treatment options for iron-deficiency anemia. Expert Opin Drug Saf. 2018; 17(2): 149-159."},{t:"p",x:"Musallam KM, Taher AT. Iron deficiency beyond erythropoiesis: should we be concerned? Curr Med Res Opin. 2018 Jan;34(1):81-93."},{t:"p",x:"Oldenburg B, Koningsberger JC, Van Berge Henegouwen GP, Van Asbeck BS, Marx JJ. Iron and inflammatory bowel disease. Aliment Pharmacol Ther. 2001;15:429–438. doi: 10.1046/j.1365- 2036.2001.00930.x."},{t:"p",x:"Pasricha, S.R.; Tye-Din, J.; Muckenthaler, M.U.; Swinkels, D.W. Iron Deficiency. Lancet 2021, 397, 233- 248."},{t:"p",x:"Rayani S. Oral iron supplements - a review. In: B Walsh, ed. The Tablet, Vol. 25. Vancouver, BC: British Columbia Pharmacy Association; 2016; 8-9."},{t:"p",x:"Rutten, F.H.; Cramer, M.J.; Grobbee, D.E.; Sachs, A.P.; Kirkels, J.H.; Lammers, J.W.; Hoes, A.W. Unrecognized Heart Failure in Elderly Patients with Stable Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease. Eur. Heart J. 2005, 26, 1887–1894."},{t:"p",x:"Santiago P. Ferrous versus ferric oral iron formulations for the treatment of iron deficiency: a clinical overview. ScientificWorldJournal. 2012; 2012: 846824."},{t:"p",x:"Shah Y, Patel D, Khan N, et al. Iron deficiency anemia in IBD: an overlooked comorbidity. Expert Rev Gastroenterol Hepatol 2021; 15:771-81. doi:10.1080/17474124.2021.1900730."},{t:"p",x:"Shiba, N.; Shimokawa, H. Chronic Kidney Disease and Heart Failure--Bidirectional Close Link and Common Therapeutic Goal. J. Cardiol. 2011, 57, 8–17."},{t:"p",x:"Sifakis S, Pharmakides G. Anemia in pregnancy. Ann NY Acad Sci, 2000; 900:125-136. WHO/UNICEF Discussion Paper: The Extension of the 2025 Maternal, Infant and Young Child Nutrition Targets to 2030- UNICEF DATA."},{t:"p",x:"World Health Organisation. Haemoglobin concentrations for the diagnosis of anaemia and assessment of severity. Vitamin and Mineral Nutrition Information System 2011."},{t:"p",x:"Yeo TJ, Yeo PS, Ching-Chiew Wong R, Ong HY, Leong KT, Jaufeerally F, Sim D, Santhanakrishnan R, Lim SL, M Y Chan M, Chai P, Low AF, Ling LH, Ng TP, Richards AM, Lam CS. Iron deficiency in a multi-ethnic Asian population with and without heart failure: prevalence, clinical correlates, functional significance and prognosis. Eur J Heart Fail. 2014;16:1125–1132. doi: 10.1002/ejhf.161."},{t:"p",x:"Zhao J., Zhu X., Dai Q., Hong X., Zhang H., Huang K., Wang Y., Yang X., Zhang Y., Peng Z., et al. The prevalence and influencing factors of anaemia among pre-pregnant women in mainland China: A large population-based, cross-sectional study. Br. J. Nutr. 2022;127:439–450. doi: 10.1017/S0007114521001148. Zohora F, Bidad K, Pourpak Z, Moin M. Biological and Immunological Aspects of Iron Deficiency Anemia in Cancer Development: A Narrative Review. Nutr Cancer. 2018 May-Jun;70(4):546-556."}],r={n:6,title:e,authors:i,pages:n,affiliations:a,blocks:o};export{a as affiliations,i as authors,o as blocks,r as default,t as n,n as pages,e as title};
